      "text": "IDFC FIRST Bank Limited Corporate Office: IDFC FIRST Bank Tower, (The Square), C-61, G Block, Bandra-Kurla Complex, Bandra (East), Mumbai - 400 051, Maharashtra. Tel: +91 22 7132 5500 Registered Office: KRM Tower, 7th Floor, No. 1, Harrington Road, Chetpet, Chennai 600 031, Tamil Nadu. Tel: +91 44 4571 6477 CIN: L65110TN2014PLC097792 | E-mail: bank.info@idfcfirstbank.com; | Website: www.idfcfirst.bank.in IDFCFIRSTBANK/SD/113/2026-27 August 09, 2026 National Stock Exchange of India Limited BSE Limited Mumbai 400 051 Mumbai 400 001 NSE Symbol: IDFCFIRSTB BSE Scrip Code: 539437 Sub.: Newspaper publication regarding dispatch of Notice of the Annual General Meeting (“AGM”), Integrated Annual Report of the Bank for Financial Year 2025-26 and e-Voting related matters. Ref.: Disclosure under Regulation 30 of the SEBI (Listing Obligations and Disclosure Requirements) Regulations, 2015, as amended (‘SEBI Listing Regulations’). Dear Sir/ Madam, Pursuant to applicable provisions of the SEBI Listing Regulations, we hereby submit copies of newspaper advertisement published by the Bank in ‘Hindu Business Line (English)’ and ‘Makkal Kural (Tamil)’, in their respective publication dated August 09, 2026, intimating dispatch of Notice of the 12th AGM, scheduled to be held on Monday, August 31, 202 6 at 02:00 p.m. (IST) through Video Conferencing (‘VC’) / Other Audio Visual Means (‘ OAVM’), along with Integrated Annual Report of the Bank for Financial Year 2025-26 and e-voting related matters. The above information is also being hosted on the Bank’s website at www.idfcfirst.bank.in , in terms of the SEBI Listing Regulations. Please take the above on record. Thanking You, Yours Faithfully, For IDFC FIRST Bank Limited Satish Gaikwad General Counsel and Company Secretary Encl.: As above Sunehra Pension Plan unveiled Generali Central Life Insurance Co has launched the Sunehra Pension Plan, a participating (PAR), non-linked, pension, savings-oriented life insurance plan aimed at helping policyholders build a retirement corpus for their ﬁnancial security. Beyond its insurance beneﬁts, the plan also oﬀers complimentary wellness services. Bonuses under the Sunehra Pension Plan begin accruing from the ﬁrst policy year. The plan oﬀers ﬂexibility to choose a policy term ranging from ﬁve to 40 years. It also provides liquidity through partial withdrawals during ﬁnancial emergencies, multiple premium payment options and the ability to enhance protection through optional riders. A BM-BMECM YK MUMBAI business line.portfolio SUNDAY - AUGUST 9 - 2026 9Safe Investing . bl . ALERTS. One of the harder parts of longterm investing is not deciding when to take risk, but when to reduce it. Suppose you are investing for a child’s higher education 15 years away, a high equity allocation may be reasonable initially. But if the stock market falls 30 per cent when the goal is only a year away, there may simply not be enough time for the portfolio to recover. This is the problem that lifecycle-based investment strategies available with several unit-linked insurance plans (ULIPs) attempt to address. If the policyholder opts for such a strategy, the insurer automatically switches between equity and debt funds according to a predetermined formula. The underlying idea is quite simple. Take more investment risk when the goal is distant and progressively reduce it as the goal approaches. Many life insurers have their version of automated lifecycle strategies. In this piece, we will discuss a few. Note that such strategies are not exclusive to ULIPs. NPS has been oﬀering it since many years, while some mutual funds are providing it from this year. HOW THE WHEEL TURNS Lifecycle strategies broadly follow the same principle, but insurers diﬀer in how quickly they reduce equity exposure and what triggers the shift. Some use years left to maturity, while others use the policyholder’s age. Consider Bajaj Life’s Wheel of Life Portfolio Strategy. In its Future Wealth Gain IV ULIP , when 20 or more years remain to maturity, the allocation is entirely to equity funds — 20 per cent to a blue-chip equity fund, 50 per cent to an equity growth fund and 30 per cent to a midcap fund. The allocation becomes progressively conservative thereafter. With 10 years remaining, equity falls to 70 per cent and bonds account for 30 per cent. At ﬁve years, equity is down to 40 per cent, while 55 per cent goes to bonds and 5 per cent to a liquid fund. In the ﬁnal year, equity drops to zero, with 80 per cent in bonds and 20 per cent in liquid assets. Axis Max Life follows the same years-to-maturity approach, but with a diﬀerent pace of de-risking through its Dynamic Fund Allocation strategy. In the Platinum Wealth Plan, the predeﬁned allocation is 80 per cent to its growth fund and 20 per cent to a secure fund when 16-20 years remain. Equity-oriented exposure falls to 70 per cent with 8-15 years remaining, 50 per cent with fourseven years remaining and 20 per cent during the last three years. So, the principle is similar, but the glide paths clearly are not identical. There is another way of doing it. Making age rather than policy maturity the trigger. ICICI Prudential Life’s LifeCycle based Portfolio Strategy 2, available with LifeTime Classic, divides investments between its Multi Cap Growth Fund and Income Fund according to the policyholder’s age. The allocation to the Multi Cap Growth Fund is 80 per cent up to age 25, 75 per cent at ages 26-35, and 65 per cent at 36-45. It declines further to 55 per cent at 46-55, 45 per cent at ages 5665, and ﬁnally 35 per cent for ages 66 and above. The allocation is automatically rearranged when the Life Assured enters another age band. There is also a maturitylinked safeguard. During the last 10 quarters of the policy, savings in the Multi Cap Growth Fund are systematically transferred to the Income Fund in 10 installments. HDFC Life, too, oﬀers an Automatic Asset Rebalancing Strategy in its Smart Pension Plan, where allocations between equity-oriented funds and a debt-oriented fund change according to the number of years remaining to vesting. CASE FOR AUTOMATION For policyholders who do not actively manage investments, such strategies can address a genuine behavioural problem. Investors frequently rebalance in the wrong direction. After a strong equity rally, they become comfortable with a larger equity allocation; after a crash, they become reluctant to hold equities. A predetermined glide path does the opposite of emotional market timing. The rule is decided beforehand and implemented irrespective of market sentiment. More importantly, it reduces the risk of a sharp equity-market decline close to a ﬁnancial goal. A 25 per cent market decline with 15 years remaining is very diﬀerent from the same decline six months before money is required for college fees or retirement. There is also convenience. A policyholder need not remember to shift money from equity to debt every few years or decide how much to move. ONE SIZE DOESN’T FIT ALL Automation should not be confused with optimisation. An age-based strategy, for instance, assumes that people of similar ages require broadly similar asset allocations. That may be convenient, but a 50year-old investing for a goal three years away is diﬀerent from another 50-year-old building wealth for the next 15 years. A maturity-based strategy aligns better with a particular goal, but it brings another risk: de-risking too quickly. If equities perform strongly during the later years, a portfolio that has mechanically moved heavily into debt will participate less in that upside. Debt funds are also lowerrisk, not risk-free. Interest-rate and credit risks remain, depending on the underlying portfolio. Policyholders should therefore examine the actual allocation table rather than stopping at names such as “lifecycle”, “dynamic” or “wheel of life”. Ask four questions: How much equity will I hold today? When does de-risking begin? How much equity remains in ﬁnal years? Can I exit or change strategy if my situation does? Finally, asset allocation cannot make a poor insurance product a good one. Before buying a ULIP , investors still need to separately assess life cover, charges, fund choices, liquidity restrictions, taxation and whether the product itself ﬁts their needs. For an existing ULIP customer, however, an automated lifecycle option can be worth examining. Its biggest beneﬁt is not that it knows where markets are headed. But, it makes sure your portfolio remembers that the date on which you need the money keeps getting closer. Kumar Shankar Roy bl. research bureau GETTY IMAGES/ISTOCKPHOTO TAKING COVER. Automated lifecycle strategies can take the emotion out of asset allocation, but policyholders should understand what they are giving up in return ULIPs that de-risk with time KEY POINTS /ring6Equity exposure falls as goals approach /ring6Debt exposure rises as maturity nears /ring6Automatic rebalancing reduces emotional investment decisions The Parliament on August 6 passed the Taxation and Other Laws (Amendment) Bill, 2026, inter alia , intending to amend the Payment and Settlement Systems Act, 2007. This move has stoked widespread speculation that the government’s aim is to introduce MDR (Merchant Discount Rate) charges for UPI, which has remained free until now. Here’s an explainer decluttering the noise around the move. What is MDR? The MDR is a fee charged by banks and payment processing companies (such as Visa and Mastercard) to merchants for each credit or debit card transaction made by customers. This fee ranges from 0.25 per cent to 1 per cent of the transaction value for debit card transactions, and from 1 per cent to 3 per cent for credit card transactions. MDR compensates banks and payment service providers (PSPs) for processing digital payments and maintaining supporting infrastructure. What is likely the impact onMDR with the passing of the Taxation and Other Laws (Amendment) Bill, 2026? Through the Bill, the government aims to amend section 10A of the Payment and Settlement Systems Act, 2007. This section restricts banks and PSPs from imposing any charge on a person making or receiving a payment using certain electronic modes of payment. These modes of payment are currently provided under section 269SU of the Income-tax Act, 1961, read with rule 119AA of the Income-tax Rules, 1962, namely, RuPay debit card, UPI and QR codebased UPI. Now that the new Incometax Act, 2025, is in force, the government plans to remove references to the old legislation and instead notify those modes of payment in a future Ministry of Finance notiﬁcation. Going forward, one must refer to this notiﬁcation to understand which modes of payment are exempt from MDR rather than to refer to the Income-tax Act. Who is currently bearing the cost of operating UPI now? Currently, banks and PSPs are largely bearing the cost. However, the government is incentivising them to an extent for person-to-merchant (P2M) transactions of less than /uni20B92,000 involving a ‘small merchant’. The FY27 Budget provides an allocation of /uni20B92,000 crore for the scheme. Although this is lower than the FY26 revised estimate of /uni20B92,196 crore, it remains well above the original FY26 Budget Estimate of /uni20B9437 crore and the FY25 allocation of /uni20B91,923 crore. Besides, NPCI, which owns and manages UPI, spent /uni20B92,270 crore in revenue expenditure and /uni20B9742 crore in capital expenditure in FY25 (latest available ﬁnancials). Will UPI become chargeable now? There is little clarity on this at this point, leading to speculation. The government’s move can be interpreted in two ways. One, given that the Income-tax Act, 2025, has come into force, the move can be interpreted as merely removing the reference to the old Income-tax Act, 1961, in the Payment and Settlement Systems Act. Two, the government could’ve amended the Payment and Settlement Systems Act to include a reference to the relevant provisions of the new Income-tax Act, 2025. Section 269SU and rule 119AA of the old tax legislation have largely been carried forward to section 187 of the Incometax Act, 2025, and rule 133 of Income-tax Rules, 2026. Instead, the government intends to notify the list of exempt payment modes itself, without referring to the taxation law. Given that payment ecosystem players and the Lok Sabha Standing Committee on Finance have voiced the unsustainable nature of the status quo and called for MDR on UPI, the government’s move in this manner may be interpreted as addressing their grievance. Nevertheless, expert opinion tilts towards the former view. According to Smita Jha, Partner at Khaitan & Co, the amendment is simply a consequential legislative measure to preserve the operative force of this provision following the repeal and replacement of the Income-tax Act, 1961. The amendment neither aﬀects the substantive zeroMDR regime applicable to UPI transactions nor creates any new legal basis for levying charges that did not previously exist. Any introduction of MDR on UPI would require an independent policy action by the Central Government through a separate notiﬁcation. Will a charge for using UPI be a dampener for users? Consumers can reasonably expect not to be charged. Even in the current scenario with cards, it’s the merchant who bears the charge. The government might even keep small merchants out of the UPI MDR purview. UPI MDR may be set at competitive rates relative to card MDR to encourage adoption. However, merchants may resort to inﬂating the prices of goods and services to the extent of the possible UPI MDR, thereby ultimately passing the cost on to the consumer. How this transpires in the long term remains to be seen. Nishanth Gopalakrishnan bl. research bureau ALLEN EGENUSE J BL. EXPLAINER. Is UPI becoming chargeable? Here’s what you need to know Understanding merchant discount rate Plots, Flats (Second Sale only) & Lands for JD in Bengaluru GBA Limits.