      "url": "https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/TEXMOPIPES_14082026165100_SENewspaperPublicationFR14082026.pdf",
      "text": "Texmo/Sec/2026-27/20 August 14, 2026 To, To, Manager (Listing) The Corporate Relationship Department National Stock Exchange of India Ltd Bombay Stock Exchange Ltd, Exchange Plaza, 5th Floor Floor 25, New Trading Ring, Bandra kulra Complex, Bandra (E) P.J.Tower, Dalal Street, Mumbai 400051 Mumbai-400001 Ref: Texmo Pipes and Products Limited (ISIN - INE141K01013), BSE Code - 533164, NSE Symbol - TEXMOPIPES Sub:- Newspaper Publication - Standalone and Consolidated Un-Audited Financial Results for the quarter ended June 30, 2026 Dear Sir/Madam, Please find enclosed copies of Extract of Standalone and Consolidated Un-Audited Financial Results for the quarter ended June 30, 2026 published in the following newspapers: 1) Free Press (English) on Friday, August 14, 2026. 2) Choutha Sansar (Hindi) on Friday, August 14, 2026. This is for the information of the exchange and members thereof. Kindly take the same on your record. Thanking You Yours Faithfully For Texmo Pipes and Products Limited Ajay Shrivastava Company Secretary and Compliance Officer Encl: As above. www.freepressjournal.innation12 Indore, Friday, August 14, 2026 IANS SRINAGAR After 35 years, the Govern - ment Railway Police (GRP) Jammu said on Thursday that it has arrested the 1991 bomb blast accused from Jammu and Kashmir's Anantnag district, officials said. Officials added that the Railway Police Jammu have ar - rested the 1991 bomb blast accused from the Anantnag district, who had been ab - sconding for 35 years. The arrested person has been identified as Altaf Hus - sain Sheikh, the son of Nazir Ahmed Sheikh and a resident of Marhama-Bijbehara in Anantnag. He was booked under the Terrorist and Disruptive Activities (Prevention) Act. An FIR under Sections 307, 302, 120(B) of the Ranbir Penal Code, Section 3/4 of Terrorist and Disruptive Activities (Prevention) Act (TADA) and Section 4/5 of the Explosive Substances Act was registered at the GRP Jammu Police Station in connection with the case. During the investigation, one accused Aman Ullah Khan was arrested. Three others managed to escape and were later declared absconders by the court. Police said that in the last two years, GRP Jammu launched an intensive man - hunt. The team traced Altaf Hussain Sheikh, verified his identity and found that he had been living under a concealed identity for decades. He had reportedly estab - lished himself as a business - man in the Bijbehara area. Following the long pursuit, the accused was arrested and produced before the TADA Court on Thursday for further legal proceedings. Officials said the arrest was made possible due to sustained efforts to track down ab - sconders involved in militancyrelated cases. Many terror cases, including the 1990 killing of Kashmiri Pandit Sarwanand Koul Premi, who was a Kashmiri poet, journalist, research scholar, Gandhian, social reformer and independence activist and his young married son, Verinder, have been reopened, investigated and killers identified. PTI KOLKATA A nurse was found dead in a washroom of the state-run NRS Medical College and Hospital in Kolkata, hours after she joined duty, the police said on Thursday. The police have started an investigation into her death, while the health department also formed a separate team to probe into the matter. Health Minister Dr Sharadwat Mukherjee visited the hospital on Thursday morning and spoke to those on duty with her the previous night. The 30-year-old nurse was on night duty at the hospital's high-dependency unit (HDU) on Wednesday. According to the prelim - inary investigation, she re - ported for duty around 8 pm and went to the staff wash - room at the antenatal ward in the Gynaecology Department Building around 10.30 pm, police said. \"When she did not return for some time, her colleagues went to check on her and found the washroom door locked. After repeated knocks drew no response, two of them broke open the door and found her lying unconscious on the floor,\" a police officer said. A doctor was called to examine the nurse, and she was subsequently declared dead, he said. Personnel from Entally police station, along with officers of the Detective Department's Homicide team, reached the hospital after being informed about the incident, the officer said. The preliminary investigation found no visible injury marks on the body, and no suicide note was recovered, he said. Several medicine vials were reportedly found near her body, prompting investigators to examine whether she had taken the vials with her to the washroom while on duty. \"We suspect that the nurse may have died following an overdose of an anaesthetic drug administered through an injection. However, our detectives are investigating all angles,\" he said. The police officer said that the deceased was known among her colleagues and patients for her cheerful nature. IANS CHENNAI Two workers were killed after a powerful explosion triggered a major fire at a licensed fi reworks factory near Sivakasi in Tamil Nadu's Virudhunagar district on Thursday, officials said. The accident occurred at the manufacturing unit in Namaskarithanpatti while employees were engaged in production work. The explosion sparked a fire that rapidly spread through parts of the prem - ises, sending smoke into the air and causing panic in the area. The two workers suffered fatal injuries and died at the scene. Their identities were not immediately released. There was no confirma - tion of other casualties. Fire and Rescue Services personnel rushed to the fac - tory after being alerted and launched an operation to contain the blaze. Fire - fighters continued to douse the flames and cool the af - fected structures amid con - cern over explosive material stored inside the unit. Police and revenue offi - cials also reached the site and secured the surrounding area to prevent residents and onlookers from approaching the factory. Police registered a case and began an investi - gation into the circum - stances that led to the explosion. Officials are expected to examine whether prescribed safety procedures were fol - lowed, how chemicals and finished fi reworks were stored, and whether production was being carried out within the conditions of the factory’s licence. 1991 Anantnag blast absconding accused held by Jammu Rly Police FLASHBACK... H e was involved in a blast that took place on April 2, 1991, at Platform number 1 of the Jammu Railway Station. The explosion killed three persons, including two paramilitary personnel, and injured 18 civilians, officials said. Two workers killed as blaze engulfs fireworks factory Nurse found dead in washroom of state-run hospital in Kolkata ¸fWcX-¸fWXf³f¦fSX VfbIiY½ffSX 14 A¦fÀ°fXXX 2026 04 ÀU°fÔÂf°ff dQUÀf IZY CX´f»fÃ¹f ¸fZÔ ¶fb²fUfSX IYû ¸ff³f´fbSX ³f¦fSX ¸fZÔ ·f½¹f d°fSXÔ¦ff ¹ffÂff d³fIYf»fe ¦fBÊÜ BÀf ¹ffÂff ¸fZÔ ³ff¦fdSXIYûÔ,dUôfd±fÊ¹fûÔ, dVfÃfIYûÔ, þ³f´fid°fd³fd²f¹fûÔ EUÔ ´fiVffÀfd³fIY Ad²fIYfdSX¹fûÔ ³fZ CX°ÀffWX´fcUÊIY ·ff¦f d»f¹ffÜ þ¦fWX-þ¦fWX ´fb¿´f¸ff»ffAûÔ ÀfZ ¹ffÂff IYf ÀUf¦f°f dIY¹ff ¦f¹ffÜ d°fSXÔ¦ff ¹ffÂff IYf Vfb·ffSXÔ·f WXf¹fSX ÀfZIÔYOXSXe ÀIcY»f ¸ff³f´fbSX ´fdSXÀfSX ÀfZ WXbAfÜ ¹ffÂff ÀfQSX ¶ffþfSX, B¸f»fe SXûOX,JbQeÊ SXûOX EUÔ AÀ´f°ff»f SXûOX WXû°fZ WXbE ³f¦fSX IZY ´fi¸fbJ ¸ff¦fûÊÔ ÀfZ ¦fbþSXeÜ WXf±fûÔ ¸fZÔ d°fSXÔ¦ff d»fE dUôf±feÊ ·ffSX°f ¸ff°ff IZY þ¹fIYfSXZ »f¦ff°fZ ¨f»fZÜ ¹ffÂff ¸fZÔ ´feE¸f ßfe WXf¹fSX ÀfZIÔYOXSXe ÀIcY»f ¸ff³f´fbSX,¹fcd³fIY ÀIcY»f, IY³¹ff WXfBÊ ÀIcY»f, CX³³f°f ¸ff²¹fd¸fIY dUôf»f¹f EUÔ ¦»fûdSX¹fÀf ÀIcY»f IZY dUôf±feÊ Vffd¸f»f WXbEÜ dVfÃfIY ·fe dUôfd±fÊ¹fûÔ IZY Àff±f IYQ¸f ÀfZ IYQ¸f d¸f»ffIYSX ¨f»fZÜ ¹ffÂff ¸fZÔ dU²ff¹fIY CX¿ff NXfIbYSX, Aû¸f ´fSXÀfUfdQ¹ff,Àfb·ff¿f ¸fWXûQ¹f,´fU³f ¹ffQU,´fbÔþ»ff»f d³f³ff¸ff,dU³fûQ þfMX,¦fûIbY»f Àfû»fÔIYe,ÀfÔþ¹f ¸fe¯ff,Ad³f»f QÔOXIY,»fûIZYÔQi Àfû»fÔIYe,´f´´fc þfMX, »ff»fc d¸fØf»f,´fiVffÀf³f IYe AûSX ÀfZ Ad°fdSXöY ´fbd»fÀf A²feÃfIY SXfþ IÈY¿¯f,³ff¹f¶f °fWXÀfe»fQfSX dQ»fe´f dõUZQe, ±ff³ff ´fi·ffSXe ¸fWXZÔQi ¸fIYfßfZ EUÔ ÀfÔIbY»f ´fi·ffSXe OXe.IZY. ´fSX¸ffSX ÀfdWX°f dVfÃfIY¦f