      "url": "https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/MMFL_17082026160620_MMFpublicationQ1FY27.pdf",
      "text": "Date: 17 August 2026 The Deputy General Manager Corporate Relationship Department. Bombay Stock Exchange Limited, Rotunda Building, P.J. Towers, First Floor, New Trading Wing, Dalal Street, MUMBAI –400 001 National Stock Exchange of India Ltd ‘Exchange Plaza’, Bandra – Kurla Complex, Bandra (E), Mumbai – 400 051 Dear Sirs, Ref.: NSE: security codeMMFL –EQ; BSE: Security Code -522241 Sub.: Unaudited Financial Results for the quarter ended 30 June 2026 - published. The published unaudited financial results for the quarter ended 30 June 2026 is enclosed for your records, as per Regulation 47 of SEBI (LODR) Regulations, 2015. It was published in: 1. Makkal Kural and 2. Business Line Please take it on records. Thanking you, Yours faithfully, For M M FORGINGS LIMITED S.MUTHUKRISHNAN Company Secretary Enclosed: as above CIN: L51102TN1946PLC001473GST: 33AAACM2164L1ZLWebsite: www.mmforgings.comPHONE: +91-44-7160 1000FAX: +91-44-7160 1010 CORPORATE OFFICE: SVK TOWERS, 8™ FLOOR,A25 INDUSTRIAL ESTATE, GUINDY, CHENNAI - 600032, INDIA. A CH-CHECM YK CHENNAI businessline.portfolio SUNDAY - AUGUST 16 - 2026 3Your Money . bl . Each quarter, India Inc’s proﬁt numbers hog the limelight, while another set of numbers — crucial for sustaining proﬁts for the long term — get lesser attention than they deserve: Balance-sheet strength. Stronger balance sheets allow for more aggressive investments which can drive growth in future. In this analysis, we do a status check on balance-sheet strength of India Inc. Over the past ﬁve years, India Inc has had to navigate an unusually turbulent economic landscape. The pandemic, geopolitical upheaval, sharp swings in commodity prices, disinﬂationary pressures and rapid technology-led disruption have all tested the resilience of companies in both the global and domestic economy. Yet, despite the scale of these shocks, corporate India has emerged with its balance sheet largely intact, helped by stronger ﬁnancial discipline and sustained earnings growth. That resilience matters because the next ﬁve years are likely to present a diﬀerent set of structural challenges. Artiﬁcial intelligence could bring disruptions of its own, energy markets may remain volatile and trade protectionism is expected to become a persistent headwind to growth. With a stronger ﬁnancial buﬀer now in place, India Inc has an opportunity to invest for the next phase of expansion rather than merely defend against uncertainty. This analysis is based on 760 companies for which all relavent ﬁnancial data (Source Capitaline) is available across each of the last ﬁve years, including 94 from the banking, ﬁnancial services and insurance (BFSI) sector, across the past ﬁve years. The data highlight not only the overall improvement in corporate performance but also the sharp diﬀerences in sectoral trends. CORPORATE RESILIENCE The strongest evidence of corporate resilience is visible in debt metrics. Excluding BFSI companies, the remaining 654 ﬁrms show a marked improvement across interest coverage, net debt-toEBITDA and debt-to-equity ratios. Interest coverage ratio (EBIT/interest cost) measuring companies’ ability to pay interest cost is at its highest point now at 5.7 times compared to an average of 4.9 times in last four years. Simultaneously, Debt to equity (0.46 Vs 0.54 average) and Net debt to EBITDA (1.4 Vs 1.6 average) which measure the debt levels of a company are at their lowest as well. This improvement has been led mainly by automobiles, and also by steel and cement in the last two years, although each sector’s progress has been driven by different factors. But Reﬁneries and Power generation sectors continue to invest in capacity with not much of a change in debt metrics. The automobile industry is in the midst of a strong upcycle. Demand for four-wheelers, higher feature content, sport utility vehicles and electric vehicles, along with the impact of the BSVI transition and tighter safety regulations, has supported both realisations and volume growth. Modest price increases have not derailed demand, and the GST rate cut enacted late last year continues to have a positive eﬀect, according to leading companies in the sector. Strong earnings have, in turn, helped automakers strengthen their balance sheets and improve debt indicators. Steel and cement have followed a diﬀerent route. These sectors have not beneﬁted from the same pace of sales growth, but they have maintained strict control over ﬁnancial leverage even while undertaking aggressive asset expansion. During FY22-26, steel reported revenue and gross block growth of 3 per cent and 10 per cent CAGR respectively, while cement recorded 10 per cent and 15 per cent CAGR growth on the same measures. Sales growth has lagged asset growth because both sectors have been building capacity ahead of future demand but have retained ﬁnancial leverage targets. The caution shown by steel and cement companies reﬂects lessons from the over-leveraging cycle of 2008-15. In the current phase, they have reduced debt even as they expanded capacity. Although leverage is now rising again, buoyant steel and cement prices, along with expectations of lower raw material costs such as coal and energy, should help support the additional debt if the operating environment remains favourable. Other sectors present a more mixed picture. Reﬁneries and power generation have not seen the same steady improvement in debt metrics. Power generation and transmission have faced strong demand, with revenue growing at 13 per cent CAGR in FY22-26, matched by asset growth of 11 per cent CAGR. With the sector expected to nearly double current capacity by 2031-32, major players such as NTPC and the Adani Group are pursuing conventional, renewable and even nuclear generation, alongside energy storage solutions. Transmission capacity is also expanding, which is likely to keep debt metrics elevated. Reﬁneries face a diﬀerent challenge. Companies, including Reliance, were on an expansion path before the recent crude price shock. If supply disruptions do not normalise, the sector’s ﬁnancials could remain under pressure, forcing companies to reassess expansion plans under more diﬃcult conditions. OUTLOOK Looking ahead, India Inc appears well placed to launch the next phase of investment. The need for fresh capital expenditure is becoming clearer as ﬁxed asset turnover, has risen closer to peak at 1.65 times in FY26. Further sales growth is, therefore, likely to require asset addition. At the same time, EBITDA and PAT margins are also near peak levels, offering companies both the incentive and the capacity to invest. With balance sheets at their strongest in years, a new capital addition cycle looks increasingly likely. Recent commentary from banks also points to credit growth being driven by MSMEs and the corporate sector. This suggests that the investment cycle may be supported by stronger access to credit as well. The nature of capital expenditure is also changing. Investment momentum is shifting from traditional heavy industries toward alternative and emerging sectors such as renewables, energy storage, AI infrastructure, data centres, indigenous defence, electric mobility and hybrid technologies. These areas are gaining priority as companies respond to energy-transition goals, digitalisation, supply-chain localisation and evolving mobility standards. Conventional energy and reﬁning players are also redirecting spending toward net-zero pathways, including renewable power, charging networks and cleaner fuel infrastructure. Additional demand pools are emerging in hospitals and hotels, where expansion is being supported by stronger cash ﬂows, wider geographic reach and healthier balance sheets. Overall, the capex narrative is becoming less dependent on traditional sectors and more anchored in alternative energy, digital infrastructure, defence indigenisation, healthcare and organised services. If India Inc can use its strengthened balance sheet wisely, the next ﬁve years could mark a shift from resilience to renewed growth. Sai Prabhakar Yadavalli bl. research bureau GETTY IMAGES/ISTOCKPHOTO CHARGED UP. With proﬁtability at a high, the next phase of investment will not only help corporates sustain it but also achieve long-term earnings growth Balance sheet is India Inc’s new source of freedom ROBUST OUTLOOK Looking ahead, India Inc appears well placed to launch the next phase of investment Assessee - A hit submit on his ITR-1 at 10.42 pm on July 20. He closed his laptop and went to bed satisﬁed. What he did not do — for the next 38 days — was verify the return. On August 27 , the portal marked it invalid. He had to ﬁle again. His story is not unusual. Clicking submit closes only the ﬁrst door. The second, third and fourth open in the weeks that follow — veriﬁcation, the Section 143(1) intimation and the refund. Each has its own rules, its own deadlines, and its own consequences for the taxpayer who ignores them. VERIFY FIRST The Income-Tax department gives the taxpayer 30 days from the date of e-ﬁling to verify the return. Miss the window, and the return is treated as never ﬁled. Interest, penalty and the refund clock all reset. The department has held this line consistently across the last three assessment years. Five veriﬁcation routes stand open. Aadhaar OTP is the fastest — the portal sends a one-time password to the Aadhaar-linked mobile and closes the loop in under two minutes. Net-bankingbased Electronic Veriﬁcation Code (EVC), pre-validated bank account EVC, demat account EVC and Digital Signature Certiﬁcate (DSC) round out the digital options. A ﬁler without any of these can print the ITR-V, sign it and post the physical copy to the Centralised Processing Centre in Bengaluru — a route the department will retire when it can, but which still counts for the paperless-averse. Veriﬁcation triggers processing. Once the return moves into the processing queue, three things become possible: The Section 143(1) intimation, the refund and — if the taxpayer spots errors or realises a loss was missed — the ability to ﬁle a revised return under Section 139(5). The revised-return route matters more than most ﬁlers realise. A taxpayer who discovers after submission that a Section 80D receipt (health insurance) did not go in, or that a capital loss was not carried forward, can ﬁle a revised return through the same portal. For AY2026-27 , the revised-return due date is December 31, 2026 — three months before the assessment year closes. It is important to note that to ensure the continuity of carry forward of losses for future set oﬀ, it is preferable to ﬁle the original return within due date. Where the revision throws up additional tax, the taxpayer pays the shortfall and interest under Section 234A/ B/C through the same selfassessment tax window under Section 140A (Challan 280, code 300) — the receipt number of the challan goes straight into the revised return under ‘details of tax paid’. THE 143(1) INTIMATION The Section 143(1) intimation is the ﬁrst formal response the department sends to a return. In earlier seasons, it arrived after months. For AY2025-26, most ITR-1 ﬁlers received the intimation within foursix weeks of e-veriﬁcation. The department must issue it within nine months from the end of the FY in which the return was ﬁled — a statutory ceiling it now beats comfortably. The intimation reads as a two-column reconciliation. The left column — ‘as provided by taxpayer’ — reprints the return. The right column — ‘as computed under Section 143(1)’ — shows what the department’s automated processing arrives at. If the two columns match, the intimation closes with ‘no demand, no refund’ or ‘refund due’. If they diﬀer, the intimation carries a demand (to pay additional tax) and the taxpayer must act. For ITR-1 and ITR-2 ﬁlers, discrepancies cluster around a few predictable heads. TDS mismatch — where the amount claimed in the return does not match Form 26AS — leads the list. Interest income mismatch, where the AIS reports a savings-account interest higher than what the return declared, follows close behind. Section 87A rebate disallowance sits third: A ﬁler who claimed the rebate, but whose total income crossed the /uni20B912-lakh mark under new-regime or /uni20B95-lakh mark under old-regime sees the rebate reversed. HRA exemption denied because of a missing landlord PAN, Section 80D limits breached and Section 80C receipts unsubstantiated on random check round out the ITR-1 shortlist. ITR-2 carries its own list. For AY 2025-26, the Finance Act, 2024 changed capital gains tax rates from July 23, 2024, so taxpayers are required to calculate capital gains separately for transactions before and after that date in Schedule CG. Filing return with only one calculation often triggers a demand. Foreign asset entries in Schedule FA, missing dividend income that AIS caught and mutual fund redemption gains not accounted for - top the ITR-2 mismatches. The response path depends on who is right. If the department is right and the taxpayer accepts the demand, the taxpayer settles it within 30 days through Challan 280, code 400. If the taxpayer disagrees, the rectiﬁcation route under Section 154 stays open for roughly four years. The taxpayer ﬁles the rectiﬁcation application through the portal; the department must dispose of it within six months. Where the taxpayer ﬁnds a genuine error — of the sort that changes the substance of the return — ﬁling a revised return under Section 139(5) is often the cleaner path, provided the December deadline has not passed. A revised return can be ﬁled anytime before the completion of assessment; however, post the December deadline, only an ‘updated return’ can be ﬁled, which has its additional tax and interest implications. THE REFUND For a return with a refund claim, the wait begins at everiﬁcation. Generally, ITR-1 refunds triggered by year of receipt under the head ‘income from other sources’. THE TAKEAWAY For the diligent ﬁler, the post-ﬁling journey rarely runs longer than a month between veriﬁcation and refund credit. For the taxpayer with a mismatch, the journey stretches across quarters. The single common thread is the discipline of tracking. A simple checklist tracking the e-veriﬁcation date, intimation date, refund credit date and a note against any Section 143(1) reconciliation ticks every box the season demands — and leaves the taxpayer with a clean record for the year that follows. The author is a Partner at Venkatesh and Co. Chartered Accountants. With inputs from Hrishikesh D. queue; the taxpayer must trigger re-issuance from the portal after ﬁxing the underlying account issue. Section 244A provides interest on delayed refund. The rate is 0.5 per cent per month, or 6 per cent per annum, simple interest. For refunds arising from advance tax or TDS, interest runs from April 1 of the assessment year to the date the refund is granted, provided the refund exceeds 10 per cent of tax determined on assessment for the year. For refunds arising from self-assessment tax, interest runs from the date the return was ﬁled. Where the delay is attributable to the taxpayer — a bank-account validation issue, an unresolved rectiﬁcation request — the interest clock stops. The interest, when paid, is taxable in the virtue of Section 87A rebate cluster around a twothreeweek cycle. Straight salarywith-TDS refunds arrived in a fortnight in the AY2025-26 season. ITR-2 refunds — with capital gains, Schedule FA or Section 89 relief (salary arrears cases) — take longer, typically foureight weeks, and where a full Section 143(1) intimation runs before the refund, longer still. Refunds arising from advance tax paid or TDS credit tend to move faster than refunds arising from self-assessment tax. Prerequisites matter. A taxpayer whose bank account is not pre-validated on the e-ﬁling portal will not see the refund credit. The same applies to accounts where Aadhaar and PAN have not been linked. Failed refunds sit in the ‘refund reissue’ Desikan Govindarajan GETTY IMAGES/ISTOCKPHOTO DE-TAX.Veriﬁcation, revised returns, the Section 143(1) intimation and the refund cycle — a guide to the post-ﬁling journey. ITR ﬁling done! Here is your post-ﬁling checklist THE POST-FILING CHECKLIST /ring6Verify the return within 30 days of e-ﬁling — Aadhaar OTP is the fastest route /ring6Track the Section 143(1) intimation for at least six to eight weeks post-veriﬁcation /ring6Reconcile the intimation line by line against the return; act on any demand within 30 days /ring6File a revised return under Section 139(5) for genuine errors or missed loss carry-forward, on or before December 31 of the AY /ring6Ensure the bank account is pre-validated and Aadhaar-PAN linked before waiting on the refund /ring6Report any interest on refund received during the year as ‘income from other sources’ in next year’s return e419824 e419824 Debt metrics of total Ex BFSI companies= |nterest coverage (LHS) = Net debt to EBITDA (lower better) (RHS)«== Debt-equity (lower better) (RHS) : __— 205 SS 1643 All units in times 120.82 ee1 C 0.40 Fa 23 24 FY25 Fyzs 00Sectoral interest coverage ratio All units in timesHFY22 MHFY26 Automobile Steel Cement Refineries Power® Generation& DistributionSectoral net debt : Sectoral debtto EBITDA pits in times | tO €quity All units in times2.0HFY22 MEFY26 HFY22 WFY26 164)3) 1.22) 0.81, -0.4ny re 0. — ny 0.0[2] g ©5F [7] g m®5=] x [= @< o | I< o 2£9 Fao 1 [=]Q 1 a A25, SVK Towers, 8” Floor, Industrial Estate, Guindy, Chennai - 600 032Ph: 91 - 44 - 7160 1000 | Ph: 91 - 44 - 7160 1010 | email: corporate@mmforgings.com | CIN: L51102TN1946PLC001473 Particulars Zin Crores ConsolidatedQuarter ended Year ended30.06.2026 31.03.2026 30.03.2025 31.03.2026Unaudited Unaudited Audited Audited1. Total Income from Operations 426.76 430.28 366.03 1605.492. Net Profit/ (Loss) from ordinary activities before tax,Exceptional and or Extraordinary items 3.07 35.40 27.73 naz3. Net Profit/ (Loss)