      "text": "Texmo/Sec/2026-27/21 August 18, 2026 To, To, Manager (Listing) The Corporate Relationship Department National Stock Exchange of India Ltd BSE Limited Exchange Plaza, 5th Floor Floor 25, New Trading Ring, Bandra kulra Complex, Bandra (E) P.J.Tower, Dalal Street, Mumbai 400051 Mumbai-400001 Ref: Texmo Pipes and Products Limited (ISIN – INE141K01013), BSE Code533164, NSE Symbol – TEXMOPIPES Sub: - Copy of Notice Published in the Newspapers for Shareholders regarding the 18th Annual General Meeting of the Company Dear Sir/Madam, Pursuant to Regulation 30 of the SEBI (Listing Obligations and Disclosure Requirements) Regulations, 2015, please find enclosed copies of notice published in the newspapers, Free Press (English) and Choutha Sansar (Hindi) on Tuesday, August 18, 2026 regarding Notice to the Members informing them about the 18th Annual General Meeting (AGM) to be held on Friday, September 11, 2026 at 12:30 P.M. through video conferencing or other audio visual means and requesting them to register Email address in order to receive Notice of AGM, Annual Report and details of e-voting through email. This is for the information of the exchange and members’ thereof. Kindly take the same on your record. Thanking You Yours Faithfully For Texmo Pipes and Products Limited Ajay Shrivastava Company Secretary and Compliance Officer Encl: As above. www.freepressjournal.innation8 Indore, Tuesday, August 18, 2026 BJP unveils Nabin’s... Apart from Madhav, three more leaders with RSS affiliation find a place in the new team: B.L. Santosh, who re - tains his position as national general secretary (organisation) and is seen as BJP’s key organisational bridge with the Sangh; national joint general secretary (organisation) Shivprakash and Saudan Singh, who are senior organisational figures with an RSS background. Former Tripura chief minister Biplab Kumar Deb has also been made one of the eight national general secretaries and he is expected to add his North-East experi - ence to the team. The party has given key roles to two of its MPs from Odisha -- Baijayant Jay Panda and Sambit Patra. While Panda retains the vicepresident position, Patra, who is a prominent national spokesperson of the party, has also been given the regional organisational responsibility for the North East. Another important inclusion into the team is that of former Punjab Finance Minister Manpreet Singh Badal as national vice-president. This move is being seen in the context of upcoming state polls as Badal had left the Congress in 2023 and joined the BJP. Nephew of Shiromani Akali Dal (Badal) founder and former Punjab CM Prakash Singh Badal, Manpreet is a five-term MLA who has deep knowledge of Punjab politics. From Punjab, the BJP has also inducted former AAP MP and strategist Sandeep Pathak as a national secre - tary. A Rajya Sabha MP. Pathak had joined the BJP along with six other AAP MPs in April this year. Former Congress Union Minister A.K. Antony’s son Anil Antony, has meanwhile, retained his position as na - tional secretary in the party. New faces from MP ... Tarun Chug, whom the party appointed in charge of its national office, is an RS MP from the state. Before the appointments, the party weighed the caste equations in the state. Patel belongs to a tribal commu - nity, and Arya to the SC category. On the other hand, Patidar and Gurjar belong to the OBC category and Singh to the Kshatriya clan. In the same way, Sharma is a Brahmin, and Agarwal belongs to the Vaishya community. For the first time, a politician from the state has found a place in the party's national media team. Politicians from MP, like Shivraj Singh Chouhan, Jyotiraditya Scindia, Kailash Vijayvargiya, Narendra Tomar, Prahlad Patel, and Rakesh Singh, have played the key role in na - tional politics until now. But a new team from MP has entered the national politics. Sharma, Patel, Patidar, Agarwal, and Gurjar have figured in the party's na - tional team for the first time. Retd judge Giribala... He said forensic reports concerning the laptop, mobile phone and Call Detail Rec - ords (CDR) were yet to be submitted. The CBI informed the court that these reports were awaited and a supplementary chargesheet would be filed after they were received. NTA in extended... The minister directed that strict compliance be ensured with the established exam protocols. He also directed that a complete overhaul of the confidential operations (CONOPS) architecture, se - cluded rooms, air-gapped systems and device deposit protocols be implemented on a war footing, the ministry said. The NTA has been directed to conduct a thor - ough audit of all exam pro - cesses and submit a report on the corrective actions taken, the ministry said. The meeting came after the NTA on Sunday night announced a re-test for three papers of the UGC-NET June 2026, English, Commerce and Sociology, following complaints about several errors in those papers. The English and Com - merce papers will be held on September 9, while the Sociology exam will be con - ducted on September 10. The NTA conducted the UGC-NET June 2026 from June 22 to 30 across 87 subjects for the award of junior research fellowship, eligibility for assistant professors and admission to PhD pro - grammes. ‘Driver’s family had... When cops went to his home in connection with the probe of the social media reel in which Saurabh is seen brandishing a pistol-like firearm, they saw the almirah was not only locked but was wrapped with tape as well. This made cops suspicious. When they opened the almirah, they found Rs 3 crore cash stashed in bags. Meanwhile, Sagar police has written a letter to the Income Tax department, in - forming it about the seizure of Rs 3 crore. Social media addiction lands Saurabh in troubleIt is from the last few years that criminal-minded youths are posting reels on their social media ac-counts in Sagar. It was two and a half years ago that Saurabh also posted such kind of reel, showing a pistol-like item. Sagar police have closely followed the social media reels and nailed down many criminals so far. It was in this connection that cops went to the house of Saurabh and found Rs 3 crore cash. 13 injured after... Crowd pressure increased on devotees who remained trapped in the barricades for several hours, causing some people to become uncon - scious. As a result, an unor - ganised situation developed for some time in the barri - cading area at Harsiddhi Chauraha and in front of Bada Ganesh Temple. Devotees whose con - dition deteriorated were rushed to the District Hos - pital. Police and the administrative officials reached the spot and brought the situation under control. However, some of the in - jured alleged negligence in crowd management and delays in receiving assis - tance. Collector Raushan Kumar Singh denied that any stampede had oc - curred and described re - ports of the incident as misleading. Till 6 pm, 8,42,077 devotees have had 'darshan' of Shri Nagchan - dreshwar Temple which is located on the third floor of the Mahakaleshwar Temple and is open only on the oc - casion of Nag Panchami. PILGRIM TRAGEDY | In Tarapith, several guests were trapped on the upper floors as thick smoke spread through the building Aritra Singha KOLKATA Eight people, mainly pilgrims, were killed and several in - jured in a devastating fire that broke out in a hotel in Tara - pith in Birbhum during the wee hours of Monday. The police have arrested the owner of the hotel and are probing the cause of the fire and also probing whether the hotel authorities had taken adequate safety measures to prevent the fire. The fire was spotted around 5 am on the ground floor of the four-storey hotel building when the guests were mostly sleeping. Twenty-two fire tenders were pressed into service and doused the fire after almost three hours. The arrested owner, Kalyan Paul, said that there were no loopholes in the hotel 'Bideshini' and also that there was an emergency exit at the back of the building. “Our staff had immediately switched off the electricity connection in the hotel as the fire was spotted. Due to darkness and smoke the boarders could not find the back gate and the reception,” said the owner. Nilesh Srikant, additional superintendent of police of Birbhum, said that the injured have been admitted to the Rampurhat Government Medical College and Hospital. A special team has been formed which will investigate the safety measures of the hotels as thousands of pilgrims are expected to gather at Tarapith soon due to 'Kaushiki Amavasya'. Thousands of people gathered at the temple town of Tarapith yesterday to offer prayers to the Goddess on the last Monday of the Bengali 'Savan' month. From front page FP News Service HARIDWAR Patanjali Yogpeeth marked the country's 80th Independence Day with a flag-hoisting ceremony at Yog Bhawan, Patanjali Wellness, Patanjali Yogpeeth-2, on Aug 15. Swami Ramdev and Acharya Balkrishna extended Independence Day greetings to citizens. The ceremony was followed by 'Vande Mataram'. Ramdev said lakhs of freedom fighters sacrificed their lives for India's Independence and citizens now had a duty to make the country a leading economic, spiritual and political power. He said the nation belonged not to a few political parties or those in power, but to 145 crore Indians. Calling for a healthy, prosperous and developed India, Ramdev urged citizens to end hatred based on politics, caste and religion and uphold constitu - tional, democratic and humanitarian values. He also reiterated his call for a drug-free India, urging people to shun marijuana, cannabis, tobacco, cigarettes and alcohol. He called for ending corruption, preventing paper leaks and ensuring equal employment opportunities. Ramdev said India must also seek freedom from economic, educational, medical, ideological and cultural de - pendence and strengthen indigenous education, yoga, Ayurveda and naturopathy. Acharya Balkrishna said people often remembered their rights but forgot their responsibilities and duties. He said fulfilling responsibilities properly was essential for India's prosperity. Students and employees of Patanjali institutions presented patriotic cultural programmes during the event. NATION BELONGS TO 145 CR INDIANS, SAYS RAMDEV BENGAL HOTEL BLAZE KILLS 8 Rescue personnel carry out operations Dheeraj Kumar LAKHISARAI At least seven women were killed and more than 12 people injured in a stampede near Ashokdham temple in Bihar’s Lakhisarai district on the third Monday of Sawan after a rumour spread about a live high-voltage wire snapping and falling on people. Lakhisarai district magis - trate Shailendra Kumar confirmed the deaths of seven devotees in the incident. He said that a sudden electric pole collapse and a rumour about a live high-voltage wire triggered panic among thousands of Ashokdham temple devotees, leading to a stampede-like situation. The incident took place after devotees' queues stretched back towards the nearby Ashokdham Halt railway sta - tion. Between around 6:30 am and 6:45 am, two incom - ing trains arrived at the rail - way halt, causing a sudden influx of worshippers and further swelling the already crowded queues. 7 die in stampede near Bihar temple UP schoolgirls protest over teacher shortage, 2nd stir in 4 days PTI / LUCKNOW Girls of a government-run residential school here staged a protest amid heavy rain on Monday -- the second demonstration by students of the institution in four days -- primarily over shortage of teachers and disruption of classes. The demonstration drew a sharp reaction from Samajwadi Party chief Akhilesh Yadav, who questioned the BJP government over children being forced to take to the streets. Uttar Pradesh minister Asim Arun said the matter related to the appointment of guest teachers was before the high court, which is expected to deliver a verdict on Tuesday. The minister, whose Social Welfare Department runs the school, said the concerns raised by the stu