n of these schemes NFL was able to only reduce losses but further tightening of energy norms will make operational of these plants unviable. An expert Committee under the chairmanship of NITI Aayog was constituted by the Government of India to review and recommend revised Target Energy Norms To meet revised Norms of NUP-2015, NFL has already spent more than Rs 1000 Crore. Negative Energy-saving projects have reduced energy consumption and carbon emissions by over 25%. However, stricter future norms could signiﬁcantly aﬀect proﬁtability and operational viability. 55 for urea units under NUP-2015 applicable from 01.04.2025. 3 Compliance with Renewable Energy Obligations notiﬁed by BEE Risk Fertilizer plants primarily rely on captive power generation through GTG-HRSG systems to ensure process reliability and avoid plant disruptions. As per Gazette Notiﬁcation dated 27.09.2025, the minimum share of the electrical energy consumption from renewable energy has been speciﬁed for designated energy consumers, who are electricity distribution licensees, open access consumers and captive users. NFL has procured total 80,128 numbers of Renewable Energy Certiﬁcates to comply with the obligations for FY2024-25. Negative Annual ﬁnancial impact of approximately ₹4–5 crore towards purchase of RECs for compliance. Additional expenditure will impact the ﬁnancials of the company. 4 Agriculture Waste Management (Composting/ FOM/ PDM) Opportunity Agriculture Waste can be utilized as Organic manure which oﬀers numerous environmental beneﬁts, primarily by reducing pollution, enhancing soil health, and promoting sustainability in agriculture. NA The organic agroproducts i.e. City Compost, FOM & PDM increase the turnover & proﬁtability of Company. Positive 56 SECTION B: MANAGEMENT AND PROCESS DISCLOSURES This section is aimed at helping businesses demonstrate the structures, policies and processes put in place towards adopting the NGRBC Principles and Core Elements. Disclosure Questions P 1 P